T928	Quotation 134287 134314	"Auntie, what have I done?"
R1163	Speaker Arg1:T926 Arg2:T928	
R1164	Addressee Arg1:T924 Arg2:T928	
T929	Quotation 134315 134775	"Well, you've done enough. Here I go over to Sereny Harper, like an old softy, expecting I'm going to make her believe all that rubbage about that dream, when lo and behold you she'd found out from Joe that you was over here and heard all the talk we had that night. Tom, I don't know what is to become of a boy that will act like that. It makes me feel so bad to think you could let me go to Sereny Harper and make such a fool of myself and never say a word."
R1165	Speaker Arg1:T924 Arg2:T929	
R1166	Addressee Arg1:T926 Arg2:T929	
T930	Entity 135012 135014	he
T931	Cue 135015 135019	said
T932	Quotation 135021 135076	"Auntie, I wish I hadn't done it-- but I didn't think."
R1167	Speaker Arg1:T930 Arg2:T932	
R1168	Cueing Arg1:T931 Arg2:T932	
T933	Quotation 135077 135388	"Oh, child, you never think. You never think of anything but your own selfishness. You could think to come all the way over here from Jackson's Island in the night to laugh at our troubles, and you could think to fool me with a lie about a dream; but you couldn't ever think to pity us and save us from sorrow."
R1169	Addressee Arg1:T930 Arg2:T933	
R1170	Addressee Arg1:T924 Arg2:T932	
R1171	Speaker Arg1:T924 Arg2:T933	
T934	Quotation 135389 135535	"Auntie, I know now it was mean, but I didn't mean to be mean. I didn't, honest. And besides, I didn't come over here to laugh at you that night."
R1172	Speaker Arg1:T930 Arg2:T934	
R1173	Addressee Arg1:T924 Arg2:T934	
T935	Quotation 135536 135566	"What did you come for, then?"
R1174	Addressee Arg1:T930 Arg2:T935	
R1175	Speaker Arg1:T924 Arg2:T935	
T936	Quotation 135567 135646	"It was to tell you not to be uneasy about us, because we hadn't got drownded."
R1176	Speaker Arg1:T930 Arg2:T936	
R1177	Addressee Arg1:T924 Arg2:T936	
T937	Quotation 135647 135813	"Tom, Tom, I would be the thankfullest soul in this world if I could believe you ever had as good a thought as that, but you know you never did-- and I know it, Tom."
R1178	Addressee Arg1:T930 Arg2:T937	
R1179	Speaker Arg1:T924 Arg2:T937	
T938	Quotation 135814 135886	"Indeed and 'deed I did, auntie -- I wish I may never stir if I didn't."
R1180	Speaker Arg1:T930 Arg2:T938	
R1181	Addressee Arg1:T924 Arg2:T938	
T939	Quotation 135887 135966	"Oh, Tom, don't lie-- don't do it. It only makes things a hundred times worse."
R1182	Addressee Arg1:T930 Arg2:T939	
R1183	Speaker Arg1:T924 Arg2:T939	
T940	Quotation 135967 136077	"It ain't a lie, auntie; it's the truth. I wanted to keep you from grieving-- that was all that made me come."
R1184	Speaker Arg1:T930 Arg2:T940	
R1185	Addressee Arg1:T924 Arg2:T940	
T941	Quotation 136078 136277	"I'd give the whole world to believe that-- it would cover up a power of sins, Tom. I'd 'most be glad you'd run off and acted so bad. But it ain't reasonable; because, why didn't you tell me, child?"
R1186	Addressee Arg1:T930 Arg2:T941	
R1187	Speaker Arg1:T924 Arg2:T941	
T942	Quotation 136278 136504	"Why, you see, when you got to talking about the funeral, I just got all full of the idea of our coming and hiding in the church, and I couldn't somehow bear to spoil it. So I just put the bark back in my pocket and kept mum."
